<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.12.3: Writing an Equation Given the Slope and a Point</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00265</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.12.3: Writing an Equation Given the Slope and a Point</md:title>
    <md:uuid>72140797-33e9-432b-a3fa-d4d839856402</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>What if the point you are given isn’t a \( y \)-intercept? Point-slope is a great form to use when you are given the slope and any point. </para>
<para>First, work to derive the point-slope formula by completing the following steps:</para>
<para>Start with the slope formula as \( m= \frac{y-y_1}{x-x_1} \).</para>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Step 1 - Multiply both sides of the equation by \( x - x_1 \).</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: \( m(x-x_1)=\left(\frac{y-y_1}{x-x_1}\right)(x-x_1) \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Step 2 - Simplify.</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: \( m(x-x_1)=y-y_1 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Step 3 - Rewrite the equation with the \(y\) terms on the left.</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: \( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Q1-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>With your partner, you will find the equation of the line in point-slope form that goes through the point \( (3, 4) \) and has a slope of 2 by answering the following questions.</para>
    <para>Use the point and slope to graph the line on a coordinate plane.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, and then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/390f352c17676b18164cb6fe769e1ef7b67d2889"/></media></figure></solution></exercise>
</example><!--Q2-->
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Write the point-slope form.</para>
    <para>Write down your answer, and then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer: </para>
  <para>\( y-y_1=m(x-x_1) \)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example><!--Q3-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Name the point \( (x_1, y_1) \) and the slope, \( m \).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: </para>
    <para><span>\( x_1=3,\ y_1=4,\ m =2 \)</span></para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Q4-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Substitute the point and slope into the point-slope form.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: </para>
    <para>\( y -4 =2(x-3) \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Now, with your partner, each solve for \(y\) to write the equation in slope-intercept form. Compare your answers for any possible errors. Come to an agreement on your equation. You may also choose to graph the line on a coordinate plane using a graphing calculator or Desmos with your final equation to check your answer.</para>
    <para>What is the simplified equation in slope-intercept form \((y = mx + b)\)?</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: </para>
    <para>\( y = 2x - 2 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Convert slope-intercept form to standard form. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(2x -y=2\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<div class="os-raise-student-reflection">
  <para class="os-raise-student-reflection-title">Building Character: Social Intelligence</para>
  <div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-gap-1">
    <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/28cebef2dd7dc23448276a6d2a6943cc8d852cb6"/></media></figure><div>
      <para class="os-raise-mb-0">Being empathetic, or sensitive to what others are feeling, shows that you care about others. In fact, empathetic people are less likely to experience anxiety, depression, and addictions later in life. They are also more likely to be hired, promoted, earn more money, and have happier marriages and better-adjusted children.</para>
      <para>Think about your current sense of social intelligence. Are the following statements are true for you?</para>
      <list>
        <item>
          My relationships make me feel good about myself.
        </item>
        <item>
          The people in my life help me be my best.
        </item>
      </list>
      <para>Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
    </div>
    
  </div>
</div></section>
